<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deva Raya I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1406">1406</a>–<a href="/years/1422">1422</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangama-dynasty">Sangama dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Deva Raya I was emperor of Vijayanagara of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangama-dynasty">Sangama dynasty</a> from 1406 to 1422. Early in his reign he was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-bahmani">Firuz Shah Bahmani</a> and had to make peace on unfavourable terms, but he later recovered ground against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>. He strengthened the army, built a dam and canals on the Tungabhadra to bring water to the capital, and patronised poets and scholars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deva-raya-i.jpg" alt="Deva Raya I" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Devaraya_I_coin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Deva Raya I was born about 1370, one of the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harihara-ii">Harihara II</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangama-dynasty">Sangama dynasty</a>. When Harihara II died, his sons disputed the succession, and Deva Raya emerged the victor. Throughout his reign he was at war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velama">Velamas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>, the Bahmani Sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaburagi">Gulbarga</a>, the Reddis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kondavidu">Kondavidu</a> and the Gajapatis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a>, the traditional rivals of Vijayanagara.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He modernised the army, improving the cavalry with horses imported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, employing skilled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> of the Turkic clans and raising the fighting capacity of his bowmen. The Persian writer Ferishta tells that his first war with the Bahmani Sultan began over a goldsmith's daughter from Mudugal in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur">Raichur</a> district: unable to marry her, the king attacked Mudugal and laid waste a few villages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-bahmani">Taj ud-Din Firuz Shah</a> then invaded and defeated him. By Ferishta's account Deva Raya gave a daughter in marriage to the Sultan's prince, with pearls, cash, the Bankapura territory, fifty elephants and 2,000 dancers.</p><p>After confusion in the Reddi kingdom he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a> to partition it, and the split of Warangal changed the balance of power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. In about 1420 Firoz Shah invaded Pangal, but his two-year siege ended in disease and disaster. Deva Raya's army massacred the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>'s troops, built a platform of their heads on the battlefield and pursued him into his own lands, destroying mosques and holy places and killing many Muslim civilians. The Sultan ceded his southern and eastern districts, and by about 1422 Deva Raya held the doab between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-river">Krishna River</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tungabhadra-river">Tungabhadra River</a>, including Pangal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In about 1410 he had a barrage built across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tungabhadra-river">Tungabhadra River</a> and a 24 km aqueduct carried from it to the capital, easing the shortage of water for drinking and irrigation that had held back the city's growth; the account of Nuniz describes these works. The Italian traveller Niccolo Conti, who visited about 1420, wrote of 90,000 men fit to bear arms and a royal city 60 miles round.</p><p>In administration he kept a secular attitude, building a mosque and a slaughterhouse for the Muslim soldiers of his army, and in about 1413 a property dispute between the Shanka Jainalya of Lakshmeshwara and the Someshvara temple trust of the palace was settled in favour of the Jains. He patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> literature: the Jain poet Madhura wrote the Dharmanathapurana at his court. His queen Bhima Devi built a temple at the Mangayi Basti in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravanabelagola">Shravanabelagola</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Deva Raya I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
